1. Mayor and Council. The mayor and city council shall have sole authority with regard to the following personnel matters:
a. Hiring and discharging all appointed employees;
b. Disciplining all appointed employees, provided that the city manager may exercise concurrent disciplinary authority as provided in the City Charter;
c. Approving, amending and abolishing the pay schedule, the city's overall organization chart, any comprehensive system of merit raises, any cost of living raises, and any seasonal payments or incentive bonuses; and
d. Setting, diminishing or expanding the services performed or offered by any city department.
2. City Manager. The City Manager shall serve as Chief Personnel Officer for the City and shall be responsible for enforcing the City Personnel Ordinance. The City Manager shall have sole authority for:
a. all promotions and demotions, except disciplinary demotions to an unfilled position or to a new position created by the City Manager for this purpose;
b. approval and amendment of job descriptions;
c. all employee transfers other than those where an employee interviews for and wins an open and publicized position other than his own;
d. creation, abolition and reclassification of positions;
e. any pay raises or reductions for individual employees; and
f. Final determination of appeals and grievances.
All such changes shall be authorized by the City Manager's signature and effective date on official authorizing forms, and by maintaining a pay schedule consistent with the City budget for this purpose, all of which shall be recorded and maintained in the City's electronic personnel system by the Human Resources Director. Additionally, the Human Resources Director shall place into each employee file a personnel action form as an unofficial record of any such change.
Nothing in this subsection shall prevent the mayor and council from creating, abolishing, consolidating, altering or modifying departments.
3. Human Resources Director. The Human Resources Director shall serve as custodian of all personnel records regarding city employees and shall maintain a comprehensive system of position control and a Position Control Plan, which shall include job descriptions, names and numbers of positions in each department, names of incumbents in each position, and the approved pay and benefits for each employee. No organization charts shall be required to be maintained other than the current overall city organization chart approved by the mayor and council. The Human Resources Director shall maintain a personnel file folder for each employee in accordance with Section 10-1, which shall include, at a minimum, all required tax documents and personnel action forms reflecting each change in the employee's job description, position and pay. The Human Resources Director shall be the sole investigator of allegations and may conduct interviews with employees as necessary in the review of appeals, grievances, and unlawful discrimination or harassment complaints reported to the City pursuant to the Personnel Policies and Procedures. Only in the event the Human Resources Director is the subject of a complaint, the assistant city manager shall have the authority and responsibility to investigate the allegations and conduct interviews with employees as necessary.
4. Department heads. Hiring and dismissals of non-appointed individuals shall be done by department heads. Department heads shall maintain a departmental disciplinary file on each employee of the department to the extent required by Chapter 8 and may discipline employees as provided therein. No department head may transfer any employee except upon the approval of the city manager. No department head may demote any employee except a disciplinary demotion to an unfilled position or to a new position created by the City Manager for this purpose. No department head may diminish or expand the services performed or offered by his city department except upon approval of the mayor and council.
(Ord. No. O-2020-24, § 2, 5-4-2020; Ord. No. O-2021-15, §§ 1, 2, 5-17-2021)

